  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how the gut microbiota and local oxygen levels shape the colonic epithelium and immune environment. At the top, the intestinal lumen contains a microbial community dominated by obligate anaerobes, with a smaller Proteobacteria sector, and these microbes produce metabolites. The main visible metabolites are the short-chain fatty acids butyrate, propionate, and acetate.\n\nThe center of the image emphasizes an oxygen gradient across the gut wall: oxygen comes from the blood vessel below and appears lowest near the lumen. Colonocytes lining the surface use oxygen, helping maintain this low-oxygen environment that favors anaerobes. Butyrate is shown acting on colonocytes and being linked to lactate and glucose metabolism, suggesting that microbial products influence epithelial energy use. Propionate and acetate, and also butyrate, point toward regulatory T cells, indicating that these metabolites promote immune regulation.\n\nOn the left, epithelial damage triggers an epithelial repair response, with a deepened crypt labeled crypt hyperplasia and actively dividing colonocytes. Overall, the figure conveys a feedback loop in which epithelial metabolism maintains luminal hypoxia, supports beneficial anaerobic microbes, and their metabolites in turn help preserve barrier repair and anti-inflammatory immune balance.",
